        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1262">

                <pb facs="0874" n="758" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg758"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.512/468.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Brazilian Minister to
                        Mexico to the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 52.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Brazilian Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Mexico City</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-10-06">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 6, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your telegraphic
                    communication 217 of August 27 last, relative to the American Mining and Milling
                    Company of Guanajuato, I have the honor to inform you, so that the company in
                    question be advised, that the Foreign Office on the date of October 1st, has
                    been pleased to advise me that proper orders were issued to the collectors of
                    taxes at Guanajuato, that no new payment shall be made on their properties,
                    including Siglo XX, Dolores, Edwin E. Harriet, Corwin and Green, and those of
                    the Consolidated Mining and Milling Co., known as Meliza and Ana Maria.</p>
                <p>With assurances [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">J. M. Cardoso de
                    Oliveira</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
